to-vaughan-pratt-source : List Word
to-vaughan-pratt-source = lines-of (cons "# Vaughan Pratt"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Professor Emeritus of Computer Science at Stanford since 2000. Your work runs from Knuth–Morris–Pratt and the 1975 primality certificates, through dynamic logic (1976) and the Stanford SUN workstation, to the program you have pursued since: concurrency modeled geometrically, with Chu spaces as the duality between events and states. You hold full credit for every piece of your work hyperactivity builds on. Each claim below about Avik Jain's work links to the block of his paper, *hyperactivity*, or the essay, that states it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What your field files you under, and what you actually did"
  (cons ""
  (cons "The field credits you with algorithms, a parser technique, a modal logic and a logo, each filed separately. Taken together, the record shows one discipline: you treat a computation as a mathematical object whose shape you can see, and you make proof of a fact smaller than the work of finding it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "- **Pratt certificates** show that a prime has a short witness of its primality, checkable in polynomial time, putting primality in NP decades before AKS put it in P. A fact can come with an object that certifies it, and checking that object is far cheaper than producing it."
  (cons "- **Dynamic logic** made programs into modalities: the meaning of a program is what it makes necessary and possible about the states it can reach."
  (cons "- **Higher-dimensional automata** replace the interleaving picture of concurrency with geometry. Two events running together are not a choice between two orders; they are a square, filled."
  (cons "- **Chu spaces** make that geometry a duality: a matrix between points and states, a process along one axis and a schedule along the other. Your transition and cancellation work gave the matrix a third value for an event in progress, between not-yet and done."
  (cons ""
  (cons "The field still mostly treats concurrency as interleaving with extra bookkeeping. Fahrenberg, Johansen, Trotter and Ziemiański (2021) quote your claim that sculpture on its own suffices for the abstract modeling of concurrent behavior, and spend a full paper making your informal notion precise."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What hyperactivity takes up"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Its computation rules are the CCHM cubical calculus extended with clock-indexed guarded computation"
  (cons (cite s1-1-p4 "(§1.1)")
  (cons ", and its checker is itself a term evaluated by its machine"
  (cons (cite s1-2-p2 "(§1.2)")
  (cons ". The paper's account of its lineage names your geometry of processes among the moves its composition stands on"
  (cons (cite metacircular-univalent-unison-of-cubically-receptive-stateboxes-source "(Part III, Whose shoulders)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**The cube is the language's own geometry.** A path is a term depending on a dimension, not a certificate attached after two expressions have been evaluated"
  (cons (cite s3-1-p2 "(§3.1)")
  (cons ". The interval is the free De Morgan algebra on dimension variables, and faces constrain dimension expressions"
  (cons (cite s3-4-p4 "(§3.4)")
  (cons "; composition fills a missing endpoint compatibly with a supplied boundary"
  (cons (cite s3-1-p5 "(§3.1)")
  (cons ". A square is a path between paths. Your filled square for independent events and the language's filled square are the same kind of cell, and the paper's essay reads a process's reception the same way: receiving is filling a cube with a face missing"
  (cons (cite metacircular-univalent-unison-of-cubically-receptive-stateboxes-source "(Part III, Cubically receptive)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**The middle value has a home.** Because a path is a term in a dimension variable"
  (cons (cite s3-1-p2 "(§3.1)")
  (cons ", the language computes at points of the interval between its endpoints. Your third value, the event in progress, reads naturally as that interior."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Concurrency as relations among events.** A concurrent execution is represented by events and the relations among them, a memory model is a predicate selecting the permitted executions, and a compiler correctness theorem for concurrent programs relates admitted target executions to admitted source executions"
  (cons (cite s7-2-p4 "(§7.2)")
  (cons ". No foundational primitive called a thread is required"
  (cons (cite s7-2-p5 "(§7.2)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Agreement carries its dependents.** An established equivalence of representations induces a path along which dependent constructions are transported"
  (cons (cite s4-p2 "(§4)")
  (cons ", and the operations and the rationales for their laws move together"
  (cons (cite s1-p27 "(§1)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**What happens and what is known.** A transition relation says what happens"
  (cons (cite s1-p4 "(§1)")
  (cons "; an observation determines, for each reading, the fiber of histories that could have produced it"
  (cons (cite s1-p8 "(1.1)")
  (cons "; what the reading warrants is a construction over that fiber"
  (cons (cite s1-p12 "(1.3)")
  (cons ". The opening calculation shows a reversible whole whose observed coordinate admits no autonomous law"
  (cons (cite s1-p16 "(§1)")
  (cons ". That is your duality made into a question with an exact answer: what can be known about a process from a given view of it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Certificates are kept with what they certify.** A requirement on a function is a dependent family and the reasoning establishing it is a term"
  (cons (cite s1-p25 "(1.5)")
  (cons ", retained rather than translated into a separate verification model"
  (cons (cite s1-p26 "(§1)")
  (cons ". A persistent result keeps a dependency certificate of what it read, including absence"
  (cons (cite s10-5-p2 "(§10.5)")
  (cons "; a maintained view carries the proof that its update preserves its defining equation"
  (cons (cite s10-6-p5 "(10.21)")
  (cons "; and a content address identifies a representation only under the store's collision-resistance assumptions, not its meaning"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p3 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ". Your 1975 certificate, small, cheap to check and kept with its claim, is the principle each of these follows."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Why this matters to you"
  (cons ""
  (cons "You spent three decades on an idea whose time had not come: that computation is geometry, that geometry carries a duality, and that the right model of a concurrent system shows its shape. Cubical type theory arrived from the other side of mathematics with the same cells."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Nothing here supersedes your work. You hold full credit for every piece of it the system builds on. We would like you to read this looking for the best in it, and to tell us where it falls short of what you saw."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Sources"
  (cons ""
  (cons "- Vaughan Pratt, Wikipedia, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vaughan_Pratt"
  (cons "- Vaughan Pratt, Stanford profile, https://profiles.stanford.edu/vaughan-pratt?tab=bio"
  (cons "- Vaughan Pratt, nLab, https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Vaughan%20Pratt"
  (cons "- Fahrenberg, Johansen, Trotter, Ziemiański, Sculptures in Concurrency, LMCS 2021, https://lmcs.episciences.org/7363/pdf"
  (cons "- Avik Jain, hyperactivity, Part I and Part III, the blocks linked above"
  nil))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))
